    "result_text": "The full build passes with the only test class green. \n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners sharing the same firstName and lastName (case-insensitively) captured at create time, before the new owner is saved.\n\nThe value is a point-in-time snapshot (\"before this create\"), so it must be persisted and read back on `GET`. I did this by adding a small, cohesive step to the `POST /api/owners` pipeline rather than growing any existing function:\n\n- **New `CountNamesakes` function** (`rest/function/owner/CountNamesakes.java`) \u2014 one small class, one method: counts existing owners matching first+last name case-insensitively and stores it on the built owner before save.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the step between `assignCode` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field with a plain getter/setter (mirroring the existing `customerCode` pattern), so MapStruct maps it to the DTO by name \u2014 no mapper change needed.\n- **`hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column (the test profile uses hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`).\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 declared `namesakeCount` on the `Owner` schema (`int32`, `readOnly`) so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\nStructural impact was kept low: no existing function body was touched except the trivial accessor addition on `Owner`; all new logic lives in one small class with near-zero surrounding complexity, and the mapper/large classes were left unmodified by relying on by-name mapping.\n\n`AcceptanceTest` (and the full build) pass: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
